{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 46, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a turbulent era marked by rising Islamic power under Saladin. His life story involves complex political maneuvering alongside military leadership that shaped the fate of the Crusader states in the Levant. He assumed power after his father Raymond II suffered assassination by the Order of Assassins so Raymond III took control while still young and faced immediate threats from neighboring Muslim forces.\n\n*Early Life and Succession Challenges*\nRaymond spent time as a hostage in Aleppo following his capture by Nur ad-Din Zangi during a campaign near Banias. This imprisonment lasted roughly ten years and provided him with deep insight into Muslim politics and culture before his release in 1173. Upon returning to Tripoli he found his county weakened and he worked tirelessly to rebuild defenses while forging alliances with other Christian nobles. His experience in captivity made him one of the most knowledgeable Frankish leaders regarding Islamic customs and strategies which influenced his later diplomatic approaches.\n\n*Regency and Political Intrigue*\nThe count became regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not rule without assistance. Raymond acted as a stabilizing force during this period yet he faced constant opposition from the Courtenay faction led by Raynald of Châtillon and Guy of Lusignan. These rivals favored aggressive military action against Muslim territories while Raymond advocated for caution and diplomacy to preserve the fragile kingdom. Tensions escalated when Baldwin IV removed Raymond from the regency due to pressure from his sister Sibylla and her husband Guy. This internal division severely hampered the ability of the Crusaders to present a united front against external enemies.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Final Days*\nRaymond eventually reconciled with Guy of Lusignan just before the disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the Crusader army faced annihilation at the hands of Saladin. Raymond commanded a portion of the forces but escaped the encirclement while most other leaders were captured or killed. The defeat led to the rapid loss of Jerusalem and many other cities across the kingdom. Raymond retreated to Tripoli where he died later that same year possibly from illness or despair over the collapse of the Christian presence in the Holy Land. His legacy remains that of a pragmatic leader who understood the limitations of Crusader power better than his contemporaries yet could not prevent the ultimate tragedy that befell his people.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.271793e-06}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0040031224}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -9.536748e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0067279255}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.066412464}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -4.5538985e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -2.1457695e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.549722e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -4.2915435e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0026050135}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.041154556}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " pivotal", "logprob": -0.023533193}, {"token": " role", "logprob": 